From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Victor <victor@local>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 07:40:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] tp: 0016 repetition guard for greedy decode

Confirmed live on the real 3-Spark fleet: pure greedy argmax with zero
repetition guard collapses into an infinite single-token loop for a real
chat-templated prompt (token 53408, "atos", repeated for the whole
completion) and for a minimal raw single-token smoke prompt (token 220, a
plain space, repeated) -- reproduced on both the new API-server serve mode
and the pre-existing --prompts-file static path. Not a regression from
either 0014 or the uncommitted multi-prompt/KV-reset work: the token-
selection call itself, kimi_k3_dist_argmax(logits, vocab), is untouched by
both. This recipe's documented benchmarks only ever check decode_tok_s /
"OK finished clean", never decoded content, so nothing before now would
have caught this -- it looks like a pre-existing characteristic of greedy
decoding on this IQ1_S (~1.56 bit/weight) quantization, not new breakage.

Adds a minimal, deterministic repetition guard applied to rank0's logits
buffer immediately before each argmax call, in both the static and serve
decode loops (they are two separate call sites in this file, so both need
the fix): any token that has already appeared N times within the last W
generated tokens gets hard-banned (-inf) before argmax runs. Magnitude-
agnostic by construction -- it acts on token identity/count, not logit
scale, so it needs no tuning against this quantization's dynamic range.
Two passes: window=1/max=1 (absolute no-immediate-repeat) plus
window=64/max=4 (caps short A,B,A,B,... cycles too).

Deliberately no RNG, no sampler, no temperature/top-p: this changes nothing
about the existing rank0-selects-then-broadcasts-to-workers pattern, only
which token rank0's own argmax is allowed to pick, so worker-side
determinism and the inter-rank protocol are untouched. Real
temperature/top-p sampling (which would need a seeded RNG on rank0,
broadcast the same way the argmax choice already is) is left as later work
if the simpler guard here turns out not to be enough in practice.
---
 runtime/examples/kimi_k3_dist_generate.cpp | 37 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 37 insertions(+)

diff --git a/runtime/examples/kimi_k3_dist_generate.cpp b/runtime/examples/kimi_k3_dist_generate.cpp
index 452fbfc..c78d96e 100644
--- a/runtime/examples/kimi_k3_dist_generate.cpp
+++ b/runtime/examples/kimi_k3_dist_generate.cpp
@@ -18,16 +18,19 @@
 #include "sparkinfer/tp/rank_protocol.h"
 #include "sparkinfer/tp/rank_transport.h"
 
+#include <algorithm>
 #include <atomic>
 #include <chrono>
 #include <cstdio>
 #include <cstdlib>
 #include <cstring>
 #include <fstream>
+#include <limits>
 #include <set>
 #include <sstream>
 #include <string>
 #include <thread>
+#include <unordered_map>
 #include <vector>
 #include <cuda_runtime.h>
 
@@ -317,6 +320,38 @@ void fill_digests(RankSpec* spec) {
     if (!spec->plan_digest[0]) spec->plan_digest[0] = 0x5A;
 }
 
+// Pure greedy argmax with zero repetition guard can collapse into an infinite
+// single-token (or short-cycle) loop -- observed on this IQ1_S quantization:
+// a full completion of nothing but one repeated token. Hard-bans (-inf) any
+// token that has already appeared `max_occurrences` times within the last
+// `window` generated tokens, in place on the logits buffer, immediately
+// before argmax runs. Magnitude-agnostic by construction (acts on token
+// identity/count, not logit scale, so it needs no tuning against whatever
+// dynamic range this quantization's logits happen to have) and fully
+// deterministic (no RNG), so it changes nothing about the existing
+// rank0-selects-then-broadcasts-to-workers pattern -- only which token
+// rank0's argmax is allowed to pick.
+void apply_repetition_guard(float* logits, int vocab, const std::vector<int>& history,
+                            int window, int max_occurrences) {
+    if (window <= 0 || max_occurrences <= 0) return;
+    std::unordered_map<int, int> counts;
+    const int n = (int)history.size();
+    for (int i = std::max(0, n - window); i < n; ++i) {
+        const int tok = history[i];
+        if (tok < 0 || tok >= vocab) continue;
+        if (++counts[tok] >= max_occurrences) {
+            logits[tok] = -std::numeric_limits<float>::infinity();
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+// Two passes: an absolute "no immediate repeat" ban on the single last token,
+// plus a wider cap so short cycles (A,B,A,B,...) can't run forever either.
+void apply_decode_guard(float* logits, int vocab, const std::vector<int>& generated) {
+    apply_repetition_guard(logits, vocab, generated, /*window=*/1, /*max_occurrences=*/1);
+    apply_repetition_guard(logits, vocab, generated, /*window=*/64, /*max_occurrences=*/4);
+}
+
 }  // namespace
 
 int main(int argc, char** argv) {
@@ -625,6 +660,7 @@ int main(int argc, char** argv) {
                 bool client_gone = false;
                 const auto t_gen0 = clock::now();
                 for (int n = 0; n < req.n_predict; ++n) {
+                    apply_decode_guard(logits.data(), dist.cfg.vocab, generated);
                     const int next = kimi_k3_dist_argmax(logits.data(), dist.cfg.vocab);
                     if (req.stop_ids.count(next)) {
                         stop_reason = "stop";
@@ -727,6 +763,7 @@ int main(int argc, char** argv) {
             std::vector<int> generated;
             const auto t_gen0 = clock::now();
             for (int n = 0; n < n_predict; ++n) {
+                apply_decode_guard(logits.data(), dist.cfg.vocab, generated);
                 const int next = kimi_k3_dist_argmax(logits.data(), dist.cfg.vocab);
                 generated.push_back(next);
                 std::printf(" gen[%d]=%d\n", n, next);
